description:
### Problem statement
We are building manufacturing functionality on top of the **Base Application**. Our extension and our users deliberately set the Location Code (and Bin Code) of Prod. Order Components, for example to project-specific locations or to a location chosen manually by the planner. When the Microsoft **Subcontracting** app is installed, it interferes with this standard Base Application process and overwrites these locations, even when the affected operation is not subcontracted at all.

When the **Routing Link Code of a Prod. Order Component is removed**, codeunit 20524 "Subc. Prod. Order Comp. Ext." resets the component's Location Code, even if the previously linked operation is **not** subcontracted and the Subcontracting app never moved the component:

- If "Subc. Original Location Code" is filled, the component is set back to that location. However, this field is filled by the OnAfterValidate subscriber of "Location Code" on **every** location change, including manual changes by the user or changes made by other extensions. So it holds the location before the *last* change, not the location before a subcontracting move.
- Otherwise the component is set to "Components at Location" of the parent item's SKU.

The same happens in codeunit 20505 "Subcontracting Management", procedure `DelLocationLinkedComponents`. It is called from the OnAfterValidate of "Routing Link Code" on the Prod. Order Routing Line (table extension 20506 "Subc. ProdOrderRtngLine Ext.") for **any** work center, including in-house work centers without a Subcontractor No. It sets all linked components to "Components at Location".

As a result, an intentionally set location is silently overwritten and components are picked/consumed from the wrong location.

### Steps to reproduce
Tested on BC 28.3 (Subcontracting 28.3.52162.52556) in a sandbox with only Microsoft apps. The logic is unchanged on `releases/29.x` and `main`. Object IDs in this request refer to `main`; in the tested build the same objects still have the former IDs 99001505 / 99001506 / 99001520 / 99001524.

1. Create locations A and B.
2. Create a routing with an **in-house** work center (no Subcontractor No.) and Routing Link Code 100. Create a production BOM with a component that has Routing Link Code 100 and a blank Component Supply Method.
3. Create and refresh a released production order at location A. The component gets location A.
4. Change the Location Code of the component line from A to B.
5. Clear the Routing Link Code on that component line.

**Actual:** The Location Code is reset to A (or to "Components at Location" if "Subc. Original Location Code" is empty).
**Expected:** Location B is kept.

Variant: Instead of step 5, change or clear the Routing Link Code on the in-house Prod. Order Routing Line. After the confirmation, all linked components are set to "Components at Location". The same reset (without confirmation) runs when a released subcontracting routing line with a Routing Link Code is deleted.

### Alternatives evaluated
1. **OnAfterValidateEvent of "Routing Link Code" (Prod. Order Component) and setting the location back:** The execution order relative to the Subcontracting subscriber on the same event is not guaranteed. By then the reset has already validated "Location Code" (SKU-dependent flushing method and bin are recalculated, reservation and warehouse checks run and may raise errors). "Subc. Original Location Code" has also been cleared already. We would have to validate the location a second time and could not reliably reconstruct the previous state.
2. **OnBeforeValidateEvent of "Routing Link Code" and manipulating "Subc. Original Location Code":** Clearing it triggers the "Components at Location" fallback. Setting it to the current location causes a redundant same-value validation with side effects and relies on internal implementation details of the Subcontracting app.
3. **`OnBeforeGetSubcontractor`** in codeunit 20505 is an `InternalEvent` and not available to partners.
4. **BaseApp events** in the OnValidate trigger of "Routing Link Code" (e.g. `OnValidateRoutingLinkCodeOnBeforeUpdateDueDateAndTime`) are raised before the Subcontracting OnAfterValidate subscriber and cannot prevent it.
5. **`DelLocationLinkedComponents`** has no event at all and is called from a table extension trigger, so there is no way to skip the reset.

### Justification for IsHandled
We need to skip the location/bin reset when the Subcontracting app did **not** move the components, for example when the previously linked operation is not subcontracted, or when another extension owns the location of the components of the production order line. An OnAfter event cannot undo the reset without a second validation of "Location Code" and its side effects, and the original value is already lost at that point. The proposed IsHandled events wrap only the location/bin reset. Due date/time handling, the confirmation dialog and all other Routing Link Code logic stay untouched.

### Performance considerations
- Event 1 is raised only when the Routing Link Code of a single Prod. Order Component is removed (`xRec."Routing Link Code" <> ''` and `Rec."Routing Link Code" = ''`).
- Event 2 is raised once per call of `DelLocationLinkedComponents`, outside the component loop, when the Routing Link Code of a Prod. Order Routing Line is changed or cleared, or when a released subcontracting routing line with a Routing Link Code is deleted. It is not raised if no linked component exists.

Both are user-driven, low-frequency operations. The publishers add no database reads. Subscribers are expected to do only lightweight checks and to set IsHandled.

### Data sensitivity review
Only Prod. Order Component and Prod. Order Routing Line records are passed. Extensions can already access them through table events. No personal data, credentials or secrets are exposed.

### Multi-extension interaction
Standard IsHandled pattern: subscribers are expected to exit if `IsHandled` is already `true`. Setting `IsHandled` skips only the location/bin reset (Event 1: of the current component; Event 2: of the components linked to the routing line) and does not skip any other logic. A subscriber that sets `IsHandled` is responsible for keeping "Subc. Original Location Code" / "Subc. Orig. Bin Code" consistent (e.g. clearing them).

### Future evolution & tests
The events stay valid if the default behavior is improved later (see "Additional context"): they still allow extensions with their own location logic to decide whether a reset is wanted. Existing tests in `SubcLocationHandlerTest` are not affected because the default path is unchanged when no subscriber handles the event. A useful additional test would remove the Routing Link Code of a component linked to an in-house operation.

### Additional context
- Code references (main @ c9376129e1):
  - [Codeunit 20524 – OnAfterValidateLocationCode](https://github.com/microsoft/BCApps/blob/c9376129e121aa17f71b803d17b01f6538c6bcd4/src/Apps/W1/Subcontracting/App/src/Manufacturing/SubcProdOrderCompExt.Codeunit.al#L57-L69)
  - [Codeunit 20524 – SetOriginalLocationCode](https://github.com/microsoft/BCApps/blob/c9376129e121aa17f71b803d17b01f6538c6bcd4/src/Apps/W1/Subcontracting/App/src/Manufacturing/SubcProdOrderCompExt.Codeunit.al#L470-L474)
  - [Codeunit 20524 – HandleRoutingLinkCodeValidation](https://github.com/microsoft/BCApps/blob/c9376129e121aa17f71b803d17b01f6538c6bcd4/src/Apps/W1/Subcontracting/App/src/Manufacturing/SubcProdOrderCompExt.Codeunit.al#L417-L462)
  - [Codeunit 20505 – DelLocationLinkedComponents](https://github.com/microsoft/BCApps/blob/c9376129e121aa17f71b803d17b01f6538c6bcd4/src/Apps/W1/Subcontracting/App/src/General/SubcontractingManagement.Codeunit.al#L130-L169)
  - [Table extension 20506 – UpdateLinkedComponentsAfterRoutingLinkCodeChange](https://github.com/microsoft/BCApps/blob/c9376129e121aa17f71b803d17b01f6538c6bcd4/src/Apps/W1/Subcontracting/App/src/Manufacturing/SubcProdOrderRtngLineExt.TableExt.al#L207-L232)
  - [Codeunit 20520 – HandleSubcontractingAfterRoutingLineDelete](https://github.com/microsoft/BCApps/blob/c9376129e121aa17f71b803d17b01f6538c6bcd4/src/Apps/W1/Subcontracting/App/src/Manufacturing/SubcProdOrderRtngExt.Codeunit.al#L265-L277)
- **Default behavior:** We also consider the current default behavior a defect. A possible fix would be:
  1. Store the original location/bin only when the Subcontracting app moves the component itself (`ChangeLocationOnProdOrderComponent`), instead of on every "Location Code" validation.
  2. Restore it only if the previously linked operation is subcontracted and the component is still at the location set by the app. Otherwise leave the location untouched, with no "Components at Location" fallback.
  3. Apply the same rule in `DelLocationLinkedComponents` and in codeunit 20522 "Subc. Planning Comp. Ext.".

  We would welcome such a fix of the default behavior in addition to the events. The events are needed in any case so that extensions with their own location logic can control the reset.

### Describe the request

Two IsHandled integration events in the **Subcontracting** app (`src/Apps/W1/Subcontracting/App`). Each event is raised directly before the Location Code of Prod. Order Components is reset because a Routing Link Code was removed or changed. The events cover exactly this reset and nothing else.

## Event 1 – Codeunit 20524 "Subc. Prod. Order Comp. Ext." (replaces the Event 1 section)

### Proposed publisher location

Codeunit 20524 "Subc. Prod. Order Comp. Ext.", procedure `HandleRoutingLinkCodeValidation()`, called from the OnAfterValidate subscriber of **Routing Link Code** (table 5407 Prod. Order Component). The event is raised in the existing else branch (Routing Link Code removed), directly before the location is restored. Because this branch is the last statement of the procedure, `exit` skips exactly the location/bin restore and nothing else. The existing code is neither moved nor re-indented. Extracting the restore block into a named local procedure guarded by the event is equally fine for us.

